# JVM options for sbt. Read automatically by the `sbt` launcher when
# sitting next to build.sbt — every line is passed directly to java
# (in contrast to .sbtopts, where every line is an sbt CLI arg and
# JVM flags need a `-J` prefix). Keep this committed — every
# developer / CI run gets the same heap settings.
#
# Why these values:
#   • -Xmx4G  — the default ~1 GB cap is too small for our multi-module
#     build. sbt's incremental compiler + Scala 3 macro expansion +
#     scoverage instrumentation regularly push past 1 GB; we'd see GC
#     thrash warnings like "11.6s of the last 10s spent in GC" without
#     the bump.
#   • -Xss4m  — bigger stack for deep type-class resolution chains
#     (tapir + zio-json derive recursive givens).
#   • -XX:+UseG1GC — pause-time-targeted collector. With a 4 GB heap
#     the default ParallelGC has multi-second STW pauses on full GC;
#     G1 keeps pauses bounded and is what's recommended for builds.
#   • -XX:MaxMetaspaceSize=1G — Scala 3 generates a lot of classes
#     (givens, lambdas, every match anonymous fn). Default Metaspace
#     can fill up across long `~compile` watch sessions.
-Xmx4G
-Xss4m
-XX:+UseG1GC
-XX:MaxMetaspaceSize=1G
